  "response": "## Solution\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem:\n\n- P(Disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(No Disease) = 0.998\n- P(+|Disease) = 0.98 (sensitivity)\n- P(+|No Disease) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\n**P(Disease | +) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / [P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)]**\n\n= (0.98 × 0.002) / (0.98 × 0.002 + 0.05 × 0.998)\n\n= 0.00196 / (0.00196 + 0.0499)\n\n= 0.00196 / 0.05186\n\n≈ 0.037794\n\n≈ **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a classic illustration of how a low base rate (prevalence) dominates the outcome despite a seemingly accurate test.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
